<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      The signature-level permission that makes the direct link possible (§16.3.1).
      Declared here, in the module both apps share, so the two declarations cannot
      drift apart — a mismatched protectionLevel silently degrades the guarantee.

      protectionLevel="signature" means only an app signed with the same key can
      hold it. Both apps sign with nexlink-release.jks (§10.5.1), which is what
      makes that work — and what makes losing the keystore break the link
      permanently (§21.6).

      The NAME is a manifest placeholder, and that is not cosmetic. A custom
      permission is owned account-wide by whichever package declares it first,
      so two variants declaring the same name cannot both be installed:

          INSTALL_FAILED_DUPLICATE_PERMISSION: Package com.thvjq.nexlink.social
          attempting to redeclare permission com.thvjq.nexlink.permission.SOCIAL_BRIDGE
          already owned by com.thvjq.nexlink.social.debug

      That is exactly what blocks having a debug and a release build side by
      side — which is the only way to test §8.4 device verification on one
      phone, and is how this was found. Each consuming app sets the placeholder,
      so debug builds get their own name and production is unchanged.

      **When :app takes this dependency for §16.3 Level 1, it must set the same
      placeholders with the same suffix rule**, or a debug NexLink and a debug
      Social will hold two different permissions and the bridge will silently
      never bind.
    -->
    <permission
        android:name="${nexlinkBridgePermission}"
        android:protectionLevel="signature"
        android:label="@string/social_bridge_permission_label"
        android:description="@string/social_bridge_permission_description" />

</manifest>
